Gateway backup trigger limitation
================================

POST /api/v1/firewall/gateways/{gateway_id}/backup/trigger does not have a
managed artifact storage implementation. Earlier versions downloaded the
configuration, discarded it, and could report a successful capture, including
for an empty result. No retained gateway configuration could be recovered from
that response. A previously returned success is not evidence of a saved backup.

The route now returns HTTP 501 with a fixed message that no backup was saved.
Its existing permission, tenant, site, deletion and existence checks still run;
inaccessible resources remain opaque 404 responses. It does not construct an
adapter, download secret-bearing configuration, or invoke a vendor mutation.
Provider messages and configuration contents cannot be returned by this path.

The separate /config/download operation remains available with its existing
controller:write and super_admin requirements. Its artifact is delivered to the
caller and must be securely retained by that caller. The /backups vendor
operation is separate and remains subject to vendor support and write policy.
Neither operation establishes FreeSDN-managed off-device backup retention.

Managed gateway artifact retention, storage selection, secret protection,
integrity verification, lifecycle policy and a tested restore workflow remain
unimplemented for the legacy trigger. This change removes a false success; it
does not claim those missing capabilities or enterprise backup readiness.

Qualification uses synthetic principals and owned PostgreSQL. Regression
adapter I/O is explicitly doubled; no customer hardware backup or restore is
performed. Existing protected download and separate vendor-route controls
confirm dispatch only, not a real device backup or restore.
